# QSaveFile commit vs cancel

`QSaveFile` writes a temporary. `commit()` atomically replaces the target **only** after a clean write. The destructor discards that temporary **only if `commit()` was never called**. Once `commit()` runs it closes the device; a failed flush/rename then cannot rely on destructor rollback and can leave a zero-length or truncated temp.

`QXmlStreamWriter::hasError()` (and other stream `status()` checks) do not report `QIODevice` failures such as disk full. Flush and check `file.error()` before committing.

```cpp
// BAD — commit() after writer-only check; device errors become a generic commit
// failure and the temp may survive because commit() already closed the file
if (writer.hasError()) {
  *errorMessage = QStringLiteral("XML writer reported an error.");
  return false;
}
if (!file.commit()) {
  *errorMessage = QStringLiteral("Failed to commit file: ") + file.errorString();
  return false;
}

// GOOD — cancel while still open so destruction removes the temp; commit only
// when the writer and device both succeeded
if (writer.hasError() || !file.flush() || file.error() != QFileDevice::NoError) {
  const QString deviceError = file.errorString();
  file.cancelWriting();
  *errorMessage = writer.hasError()
      ? QStringLiteral("XML writer reported an error.")
      : QStringLiteral("Failed to write file: ") + deviceError;
  return false;
}
if (!file.commit()) {
  *errorMessage = QStringLiteral("Failed to commit file: ") + file.errorString();
  return false;
}
```

Do not call `close()` on `QSaveFile`. Do not enable `setDirectWriteFallback(true)` for exports: cancel cannot restore a directly overwritten target.
